#{[17592186585088 "Since $\\sqrt[6]{x}={x}^{1/6}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/6+1}}{(\\frac{1}{6}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/6}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{6{x}^{7/6}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}{x}^{7/6-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/6}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186587304 "Since $\\sqrt[4]{x}={x}^{1/4}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186592302 "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186593223 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}}+\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x \\\\  & =({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x(\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+1)=1+\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"] [17592186593227 "First, we factor $2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}={\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}x-1\n$$"] [17592186600830 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/27/27a82956e22b5b2e2de2c02d9d93fb7b7ce57a2b581b88458964eda465e8bebd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1={r}^{2}+1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+1}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186604621 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186614572 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t=1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t & =1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}t & =\\sqrt{1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, substituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.$"] [17592186616135 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,4),B(4,2)$ and $C(5,7).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (4) & =(2)+2 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (2) & =(4)+2 \\\\ 2 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (7) & =(5)+2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."] [17592186616175 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd1eea07781890c1dd539e2e36f796d9b18bb4b05b33a903279b88b329f9082.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1.$ \nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(0)+1 \\\\ 4 & =1\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(0) \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0) & =\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186616198 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,7)$, $B(4,3)$ and $C(2,-5).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (7) & =-3(-2)+1 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (3) & =-3(4)+1 \\\\ 3 & =-11\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (-5) & =-3(2)+1 \\\\ -5 & =-5\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ does not lie on the line."] [17592186617022 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =36({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =36(1) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$"] [17592186620824 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & x \\\\ 3 & 2 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & x \\\\ 2 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2x)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-3x)\\mathbf{j}+(4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2x,x,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2x)^{2}+{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+16} & =\\sqrt{61} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+16 & =61 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =45 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 3.$"] [17592186620854 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-6)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-12)\\mathbf{j}+(x-6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,12-2x,x-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(12-2x)^{2}+(x-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-48x+4{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-12x+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-60x+180}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-50x+180} & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+180 & =80 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-60x+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2,$ $x=10.$"] [17592186622804 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}-6,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}9⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{3})(-\\sqrt{3})+(-6)(5)+(0)(9) \\\\  & =3-30+0 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622816 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{p},$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{p}\\times \\mathbf{q}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)-4(6\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)-2(6\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =-8+72+40 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622827 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,7,5⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+7\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =7-14-15 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186622835 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =⟨2,5,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,3⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+5\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+30-3 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660918 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-4\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}((-3)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,20,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,2,7⟩\\cdot ⟨8,20,1⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot 8+2\\cdot 20+7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+40+7 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186670092 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{9}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"] [17592186674190 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674193 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674204 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=1(((125)+\\left(- \\frac{125}{3}\\right))-((-125)+\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{500}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674214 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute 