ude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."] [17592186705614 "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a u